  "answer_text": "Here is a helpful guide on managing cold symptoms with medicine. Keep in mind that the common cold has no cure but should improve on its own. The goal of medication is to relieve symptoms and help you feel more comfortable. Here are the main types of OTC (over-the-counter) medicines you can consider:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc8a 1. Pain Relievers & Fever Reducers\nAcetaminophen (Tylenol) and ibuprofen (Advil, Motrin) help lower fever and relieve muscle aches. They can also relieve cold-related symptoms such as sore throat, headache, and earache.\n\n> \u26a0\ufe0f Aspirin can also treat a cold, but it may have potential serious side effects, such as stomach bleeding, so it's best to avoid it. Aspirin should **never** be used in children and teens due to the risk of Reye syndrome.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\udd27 2. Nasal Decongestants\nNasal decongestants help unclog a congested nose. They work by narrowing the blood vessels in the lining of your nose so that the swollen tissue shrinks and decreases mucous production.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f 3. Antihistamines\nOver-the-counter antihistamines, such as chlorpheniramine and diphenhydramine, may relieve cold symptoms for one or two days. Antihistamines such as diphenhydramine or doxylamine can cause drowsiness, so they're best for nighttime use.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\udec1 4. Cough Suppressants\nThe most common OTC cough suppressant is dextromethorphan. It can help reduce the urge to cough.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddf4 5. Expectorants\nExpectorants help thin and loosen mucus, making it easier to cough up when you have a wet cough. Guaifenesin is a common ingredient.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc8a 6. Combination Cold Medicines\nSome medicines mix ingredients such as antihistamines, decongestants, and/or pain relievers with cough medicine. Check whether your OTC cold medicine has an AM/daytime or PM/nighttime version. The daytime medicine might not make you sleepy.\n\n---\n\n### \u274c Do NOT take antibiotics\nAntibiotics don't work against viruses and won't help you feel better. Antibiotics only fight bacteria, so they don't help in the treatment of simple common colds caused by viruses. They can have side effects too, so they should only be used if a bacterial infection develops as a complication of the cold.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcdd Key Takeaway\nThe drug that you choose will depend on your specific symptoms. Talk with your pharmacist or doctor about specific products that are safe to combine to address your symptoms.\n\n> **Disclaimer:** This information is for general guidance only and is not a substitute for professional medical advice. Please consult your doctor or pharmacist before taking any medication.",
